6. Protect the character assets

The sprite system is one of the most sensitive parts of Alva.

The repository contains approximately 894 asset files, mostly PNG animation frames.

These files are lightweight individually, but numerous.

Do not assume they should be combined.

The existing file-count target can likely be reached by bundling JavaScript and CSS alone.

Preserve exact sprite behavior

Maintain:

* Frame ordering.
* Animation FPS.
* Loop behavior.
* Sprite dimensions.
* Pixel transparency.
* Pixel colors.
* Collision-independent visual anchors.
* Fighter visual scale.
* Per-frame offsets.
* Animation restart behavior.
* Projectile animations.
* Effect animations.
* Summon and ensemble animation behavior.

Alva’s sprite normalizer reads image pixels and derives information from them.

Changing image dimensions, transparency, or pixel values may alter how sprites are processed and displayed.

Do not introduce sprite atlases, image repacking, or format conversion unless they are genuinely necessary and can be proven visually equivalent.

The default solution should retain the existing PNG files unchanged.

Duplicate assets

The repository contains some byte-identical sprite files.

Investigate these, but do not automatically remove them.

Two identical images may serve different animation names and have meaningful separate references.

If deduplication is considered, preserve every logical animation frame and its original ordering.

A repeated image may be intentionally repeated to create an animation hold.

Never eliminate that timing behavior.

Do not remove any animation merely because it appears redundant.

