17. Definition of success

This task is complete only when:

* The original development source remains maintainable.
* Every existing game mode is preserved.
* Every fighter and stage remains available.
* All combat rules, timing, and physics remain unchanged.
* All animations and art display correctly.
* All supported inputs continue working.
* Both languages continue working.
* Saved settings remain compatible.
* The original test suite passes.
* The new production build has been independently tested.
* The distribution is within itch.io HTML5 limits.
* A valid release ZIP is generated.
* No unauthorized gameplay or design changes have been introduced.

The ideal outcome is to reduce the deployment from roughly 1,060 files to around 900 by bundling the JavaScript and CSS, without needing to merge the original PNG animation frames or rewrite gameplay code.

This is a target, not a requirement to force a specific file count.

Prefer correctness and simplicity over aggressive reduction.

Final instruction

Proceed carefully and implement the solution.

Start with a repository audit and baseline tests. Then create the smallest safe production consolidation, validate it, test the original and production versions, and generate the final distribution.

Do not stop after providing recommendations.

If a consolidation creates a gameplay difference, revert or correct it before continuing.

Do not consider fewer files to be evidence of success by itself.

The objective is the same Alva, with the same gameplay, same visuals, same controls, and same content — simply packaged more efficiently and ready to publish as a browser game.
